User prompt: rate and review # Planet-9 / TEP -- a field boundary in the outer solar system Reinterpretation of the extreme-TNO orbital clustering under the Temporal Equivalence Principle: a spatially fixed proper-time field gradient (a domain boundary) rather than a moving point mass. All results below are computed from real catalog data -- the complete JPL Small-Body Database outer-solar-system orbit catalog plus the characterized OSSOS ensemble. No synthetic data. ## Data - `data/sbdb_outer_ss.json` -- all SBDB asteroids with a >= 30 AU (7299 objects: orbital elements, orbit class, condition code, data arc, observation span) via `sbdb_query.api`. - `data/sbdb_centaurs.json` -- all class-CEN objects (1050). - `data/sbdb_comets_ext.json` -- comets with q >= 5 AU (268). - `data/ossos_t3char.vot` -- OSSOS characterized ensemble, VizieR J/ApJS/236/18 (Bannister+ 2018): 840 objects with orbital elements and discovery RA/Dec, distance, magnitude. - `data/warsaw_tablec.dat` -- Warsaw Catalogue of cometary orbits (Krolikowska 2014, J/A+A/567/A126), table C: original barycentric orbital elements at 250 AU inbound for 119 near-parabolic comets, with formal uncertainties. The proper Oort-spike dataset -- pre-planetary-perturbation orbits, not osculating elements. ## Steps - `steps/step_01_download.py` -- SBDB queries. - `steps/step_02_clustering.py` -- the clustering anomaly, with a conditioned null (observed a,e,i held fixed; node and perihelion argument drawn uniform) so the test isolates the clock-sector elements exactly where a temporal-field boundary acts. - `steps/step_03_discriminators.py` -- field-wall vs point-mass discriminators D1-D7. - `steps/step_04_kuiper_cliff.py` -- the belt's outer edge and the detachment structure. - `steps/step_05_one_map.py` -- do the solar system's anomalous axes share one structure? - `steps/step_06_ossos_bias.py` -- the discovery-bias control using the OSSOS characterized ensemble. - `steps/step_07_warsaw_comets.py` -- the directional test: aphelion directions from original barycentric orbits vs the TNO axis, with isotropic and galactic-tide-weighted nulls. - `steps/step_08_clock_channel.py` -- the TEP-correct comet test: a temporal boundary perturbs a comet's clock, not its arrival direction. Tests the osculating-to-original orbit discrepancy (the back-integration under standard dynamics is biased for boundary crossers), original-energy structure, and NG-solution need, all as functions of angular distance to the axis. - `steps/step_09_nested_field.py` -- the layered/nested test: multi-axis scan (TNO, ISM, CMB apex/antapex, galactic axes), element decomposition, shell-depth ordering, and the prograde/retrograde orbital-dynamics discriminator. - `steps/step_10_proper_clock.py` -- the proper clock-channel decomposition: three-leg orbit comparison (osc->orig, osc->fut, orig->fut) isolating which leg carries the discrepancy, NG recoil components, CMB/ISM velocity projection, rotation-axis coherence, epoch trend. - `steps/step_11_code_confirm.py` -- independent confirmation against the CODE catalogue (osculating/original/future orbit solutions). The `b`-series is the TNO-side bias/robustness audit (separate namespace to keep the numbered comet-channel sequence clean): - `steps/step_b1_empirical_null.py` -- replaces the uniform angular null with the catalog's own discovery filter: (Om, w) pairs resampled from the 1181-object control population, plus a sweep over perihelion-proximity coupling calibrated on OSSOS. - `steps/step_b2_radial_profile.py` -- where the clustering turns on: sliding-window and threshold scans of varpi/omega/Omega in a, aphelion, and q; change-point structure; per-bin mean directions. - `steps/step_b3_robustness.py` -- leave-one-out jackknife, discovery-era splits (pre/post the 2014/2016 announcements), orbit-quality splits, epoch audit, and multiple-testing accounting over the whole pipeline. - `steps/step_b4_pole_geometry.py` -- eigenstructure of the detached orbit poles: point-cluster vs great-circle (girdle) discrimination and the common-plane fit. - `steps/step_b5_discovery_footprint.py` -- the sample's own discovery footprint reconstructed from real metadata: every provisional designation encodes its discovery half-month, which fixes the opposition longitude each object was found near. - `steps/step_b6_antipode_deficit.py` -- the far side of the sky: is there a deficit opposite the cluster (repulsion signature), and do anti-axis objects form a different population? - `steps/step_b7_plane_warp.py` -- the radial warp: coherent mean-plane tilt per a-bin and in sliding windows, calibrated against the empirical footprint null. - `steps/step_b8_footprint_conditional.py` -- the distance-aware null: measures how the discovery footprint itself narrows with a, then rebuilds the clustering and pole-tilt nulls conditioned on each object's own measured footprint position. - `steps/step_b9_axis_coupling.py` -- what does the axis couple to? Physical-element coupling (q, e, i, a), anti-deficit robustness across era/quality splits, and the axis position in invariable-plane and galactic frames. - `steps/step_b10_proxy_validation.py` -- validates the opposition-longitude proxy against REAL discovery RA/Dec in the 840-object OSSOS ensemble, then applies the measured true coupling as the tightest data-driven pointing ceiling. - `steps/step_b11_population_selectivity.py` -- which populations carry the axis: detached vs plunging high-a orbits vs the inner belt, in 3-D perihelion vectors; plus split-half axis recovery (the TNO-side analog of step_13). - `steps/step_b12_antipodal_populations.py` -- the bipolar test: Jupiter-family comets cluster near the ANTI-axis (lam ~ 220-225 deg vs anti-axis 229) while detached TNOs cluster at +49; the full footprint machinery decides whether this is a real antipodal population datum or pointing. - `steps/step_b13_patch_scale.py` -- the angular scale of the structure: cap-radius significance profiles, mixture-fit intrinsic width, direction stability, and the 3-D check -- measured for direct comparison with the comet patch. ## Results ### Evidence at a glance The complete test matrix. All data are real catalogue orbits; every p-value is computed in the corresponding step script. | # | Test | Sample | Statistic | p | Result | |---|------|--------|-----------|---|--------| | 1 | TNO varpi clustering, conditioned null | 44 detached TNOs | Rayleigh R = 0.33 | 0.007 | real, directional (m=1) | | 2 | Measured per-object footprint null | same | conditional R | 0.035 | pointing insufficient | | 3 | Maximal-pointing ceiling | same | R_ceiling 0.24-0.30 | -- | fails on amplitude AND direction | | 4 | Warsaw osc->orig leg (discovery) | 60 full-arc comets | cap-60 median shift | 0.0089 | inbound-leg anomaly | | 5 | Warsaw retrograde discriminator | same | retro vs prograde | 0.023 | non-planetary ordering | | 6 | CODE-only orig->fut (confirmation) | 54 indep. comets | cap-60 median shift | 0.0030 | replicates at same axis | | 7 | CODE-only 8-axis scan | same | d_of contrast | all >= 0.16 | axis-unique | | 8 | Energy channel | same | |daa| cap | 0.69 | rotation WITHOUT energy | | 9 | Rotation-per-kick | same | d_of/|daa| | 0.017 | non-planetary signature | | 10 | Doubly-matched control (q + kick) | 19 pairs | matched d_of | 0.0019 | geometry dead | | 11 | Continuous, no cap, no cuts | 131 class-1 | Spearman rho = -0.24 | 0.005 | cap-independent | | 12 | Permutation null | 54 | 20k shuffles | 0.0064 | pairing unusual | | 13 | Look-elsewhere | 2232 sky dirs | fraction <= p_obs | 0.27% | one patch, at the axis | | 14 | Free axis recovery | 54 | best-fit axis | 24 deg from TNO | converges | | 15 | Split-half blind | 500 reps | axis within 45 deg | 70% | vs ~1.5% chance | | 16 | Leave-one-out | 19 in-cap | p range | [0.0013-0.0058] | no single driver | | 17 | Depth selectivity | q>=3.1 control | cap contrast | 0.16 | deep plungers only | | 18 | Latitude strata | 54 | high-|beta| stratum | 0.0017 | ecliptic confound dead | | 19 | Era stability | 54 | pre-2006 | 0.0095 | not survey-era | | 20 | Anti-cap deficit | 54 | 6/54 vs ~13.5 | ~2.4 sigma | repulsion side, mirrors TNO | | 21 | JFC bipolar population | JFCs | varpi mean ~ 220-225 | 4-10 deg off anti-axis | inflow/outflow on one axis | | 22 | Planetary baseline residual | 131 / 54 | cap after baseline | 0.0036 / 0.0018 | anomaly survives Jupiter subtraction | | 23 | Residual continuous | 131 | Spearman resid | 0.0012 | monotone decay, baseline removed | Nulls that bound the model: CMB/ISM velocity projections, rotation-axis coherence, signed repulsion push, epoch trend, NG-need fraction (lower in-cap), galactic-tide-weighted directional test. ### The anomaly, reproduced (`results/step_02_clustering.json`) Detached population (a>150 AU, q>30 AU, condition_code<=3, N=44): longitude of perihelion clusters about ecliptic lambda = 49.9 deg, beta = -17.0 deg with conditioned-null significance p = 7.3e-3 (varpi), p = 5.2e-3 (perihelion unit vectors), p = 1.2e-2 (orbit poles). The a>250 AU subset (N=26) gives the same axis (lam=34 deg) at p = 2.7e-2. Orbit quality matters: including condition_code>3 objects (many with <100-day arcs) dilutes the sample to p=0.55 -- the anomaly lives in the secure multi-opposition orbits, as the literature requires. ### Discriminators (`results/step_03_discriminators.json`) - D1 element selectivity: the anomaly is distributed across all clock-sector elements (Om p=8.7e-3, w p=4.9e-3, varpi p=7.3e-3); w -- the original Trujillo & Sheppard element -- is the single strongest. - D2 structure: uniform + von Mises fit gives clustered fraction f = 0.43 centered at mu = 41 deg. - D3 membership predictor: alignment with the axis shows no correlation with a, q, aphelion, or i (Spearman p > 0.8 throughout; AUC ~ 0.45-0.59). Shepherding predicts confinement should strengthen with a; a crossing boundary does not. - D4 resonant substructure: for every trial perturber semimajor axis 200-1500 AU the observed a-set sits no nearer mean-motion resonances than resampled nulls (max z = 0.0, global p = 0.94). No resonance-sticking signature -- the expected shepherding fingerprint is absent. - D5 no sharp gap in varpi (p = 0.74). - D6 cross-population: Centaurs (a>30, N=6) null as expected; q>=5 comets (N=268) show a real perihelion-direction anisotropy (p = 3e-7) whose axis (lam=219.5, beta=+30.8) lies 17 deg from the anti-cluster axis. Per class: JFc (N=65) p=4e-9 at (219,1); HYP hyperbolic/inbound (N=115) p=3e-3 at (200,48); discovery geometry is not modeled -- descriptive, not definitive. - D7 harmonic decomposition: the anomaly is directional (m=1, p=7.4e-3), not axial (m=2, p=0.081) -- a vector signature, consistent with a lapse gradient (or a point mass), not a quadrupolar tide. ### Edges (`results/step_04_edges.json`) - Kuiper cliff, measured: cold belt (i<5) edge at 46.9 +- 0.8 AU with width ~0.4 AU -- a sharp cutoff; hot belt (i>=5) edge at 48.0 +- 0.4 AU. Consistent with the published cliff near 47-50. - Detachment structure for a>150 AU: inner boundary q10 = 34.3 AU [33.6-35.0]; a relative density minimum at 48-62 AU (2 objects) separates the detached belt from the sednoid group (Sedna, Leleakuhonua, 2012 VP113, 2023 KQ14) at q>62 AU. The deficit is real in the census but not formally significant against smooth models (p=0.28) -- reported descriptively. ### One map (`results/step_05_one_map.json`) - Triple-axis test: the three axial anomalies a solar-system field boundary would produce -- the TNO perihelion axis, the outer-comet perihelion axis, and the heliosphere/ISM axis (Bzowski+ 2015, lam=255.8, beta=+5.16) -- are mutually concentrated at <|cos|> = 0.859, p = 0.031. Pairwise separations 16.7-42.6 deg. - The TNO common-orbit-plane pole is tilted ~6 deg off the ecliptic pole toward lam = 50 deg -- the same azimuth as the perihelion clustering axis: one coherent three-dimensional structure, not two independent anomalies. - Honest nulls: the full 13-axis set is not significantly concentrated (axial |cos| p = 0.53); the cosmological axes (CMB dipole, axis-of-evil, radio dipole, alpha dipole) form a separate grouping ~60 deg away and are not part of the local structure. ### Comet tests (`results/step_07_warsaw_comets.json`, `results/step_08_clock_channel.json`) Directional channel (step_07) -- null, as it should be under TEP: a temporal boundary perturbs clocks, not which direction comets arrive from. Oort-spike aphelia (0 < 1/a_orig < 100e-6/AU, N=110): mean direction lam=54.7, beta=-14.2 (~20 deg off the TNO axis) but no significant excess (p=0.14 isotropic, p=0.49 tide-weighted). Dynamically-new subset null. The earlier ~10 deg CometEls alignment was an osculating-elements artifact. Clock channel (step_08) -- the TEP-correct observable, marginal but consistent: - Comets arriving inside the 60 deg cap around the axis show ~1.5x larger osculating-to-original direction corrections: full-arc orbits (best-measured) median 0.105 deg in-cap vs 0.063 deg outside, p = 0.0089; all-spike p = 0.034. - Axis-specific: galactic-pole and anti-axis controls null (p = 0.77, 0.88). - Cap-localized, not a smooth gradient (Spearman rho ~ 0) -- matches a boundary patch, not a monopole. - Robust to standard confounds: correction size is uncorrelated with ecliptic latitude (p=0.96), inclination (p=0.66), q; in-cap comets have LARGER q (shallower planetary plunges), which should mean smaller perturbation -- the excess runs against the standard direction; it holds independently in both latitude strata (p = 0.048 low-lat, 0.040 high-lat). - Orbit quality makes it STRONGER, not weaker (full-arc only gives the best p) -- opposite of an astrometric artifact. - NG-undetermined comets (orbits standard dynamics cannot fit even with non-gravitational parameters): 3 of 6 sit within 40 deg of the axis (C/2001 Q4 at 12 deg, C/2007 N3 at 17 deg, C/2003 K4 at 39 deg) -- n too small for a formal claim. - Signed energy channel (new comets): near-axis comets show more negative delta(1/a) -- larger energy corrections -- rho = -0.21, marginal. Caveats: 60 deg cap radius was selected among {30,45,60}; multiple-comparison inflation means effective p ~ 0.05-0.1. This is a defined anomaly, not a detection -- but it is now the right kind of anomaly: the standard-dynamics orbit reconstruction is systematically less reliable precisely for comets that arrive through the boundary direction, and the comets standard dynamics fails hardest sit closest to the axis. ### Nested-field structure (`results/step_09_nested_field.json`) Layered-domain and orbital-dynamics tests sharpen the anomaly: - Axis specificity across an 8-axis scan (TNO, anti-TNO, ISM inflow, CMB apex/antapex, galactic center, galactic pole, ecliptic pole): ONLY the TNO axis carries the ddir excess (p = 0.0089; every other axis p >= 0.11). The discrepancy is a solar-system-domain boundary effect -- the CMB-dipole (cosmological field rest frame) and ISM-inflow axes are null, consistent with a local nested domain rather than a cosmic one. - Orbital-dynamics discriminator: retrograde comets (i > 90 deg, higher relative velocity -> weaker planetary impulse) show the cap excess at p = 0.023 with LARGER effect size than prograde (0.110 vs 0.064 in/out). Planetary perturbation predicts the opposite ordering -- prograde orbits linger near planets and receive bigger kicks. A retrograde-leading excess is non-planetary. - Layered depth: in-cap correction grows monotonically with Oort depth -- median ddir 0.087 deg (1/a in 0-20e-6), 0.102 deg (20-50), 0.115 deg (50-100, deepest origin). Comets that crossed more nested layers show larger reconstruction discrepancies -- the layer-traversal prediction. - Element decomposition: the excess is a distributed orbit-plane rotation -- no single element carries it (all p >= 0.16), but the perihelion-point direction moves. A temporal boundary rotating the inferred orbital plane, not shifting energy or eccentricity, is the geometric signature expected when the back-integration crosses a lapse discontinuity. ### Proper three-leg decomposition (`results/step_10_proper_clock.json`) Separating the correction into inbound vs outbound legs (the test that should have been run first): - osc->orig (inbound leg): in-cap excess, full-arc p = 0.0089. - osc->fut (outbound leg): flat, p = 0.60. - orig->fut (full pass): flat, p = 0.44. - Per-comet asymmetry (d_so - d_sf): in-cap median +0.0005 vs out-cap -0.018 -- in-cap osculating orbits are displaced preferentially from the ORIGINAL end. The anomaly lives in the inbound reconstruction only. - Not an arc artifact: in-cap comets have the most complete data ('full' arcs both sides of perihelion); partial-arc comets sit outside the cap. - Fresh channel, same conclusion: extreme out-of-plane NG recoils cluster within 40 deg of the axis -- 4 of 14 significant A3 outliers (binomial p = 0.07 vs 12% expected), including the most extreme normal recoil in the catalogue (C/2007 N3, A3/sigma = -70, at theta = 17 deg). Radial recoil is normal near the axis -- the unexplained push is perpendicular to the orbit plane, the domain-wall force direction. - Nulls that bound the model: CMB-dipole and ISM velocity projections (no cosmic-frame motion signal), rotation-axis coherence, rotation-per-kick ratio, epoch trend, |A3| magnitude overall, NG-need fraction. Coherent picture: the inbound leg carries an extra twist that the outbound leg does not mirror -- the signature of a localized structure crossed on ingress. The comet's clock is perturbed on the way in; the osculating orbit is consistent with its future (egress fine) but inconsistent with the standard back-integrated original (ingress carried an unmodelled lapse segment). ### Independent confirmation on CODE (`results/step_11_code_confirm.json`) The Warsaw result was the discovery; the confirmation is pre-registered on the independent CODE catalogue (~370 comets, same group, extended sample): axis (lam 34, beta -13), cap 60 deg, matched regime (Oort-spike 1/a, q < 3.1 AU, class-1 orbits). The CODE-only subset -- 54 comets that were NOT in the discovery catalogue -- is the confirmation sample: - orig->fut rotation (the pure propagated-orbit channel, no measured-orbit involvement): in-cap median 0.212 deg vs 0.131 out -- excess at p = 0.0030. - Axis-unique: of 8 physically-motivated axes (TNO, anti-TNO, ISM inflow, CMB apex/antapex, galactic centre/pole, ecliptic pole) only the TNO boundary axis carries the excess; every control axis p >= 0.16. - The energy channel is flat: |Delta(1/a)| in-cap p = 0.69. The excess is rotation WITHOUT energy exchange -- planetary kicks cannot produce that decomposition (a gravitational impulse rotates the orbit and kicks the energy together). - Rotation-per-kick ratio doubled in-cap: p = 0.017 on the independent sample -- the non-planetary diagnostic designed in step_10 hits on fresh data. - Warsaw-overlap subsample retains the discovery-leg signature (d_so p = 0.016): the two samples tell a consistent story. ### Consolidation (`results/step_12_consolidate.json`) Stress tests and extensions on the same independent data: - Cap-radius sensitivity: the excess peaks at ~60 deg (p = 0.003) and dilutes monotonically (75 deg p = 0.007; 90 deg p = 0.20). A structure with a characteristic angular scale ~60-75 deg -- a boundary patch, not a monopole or a smooth gradient. In-cap effect size stable (med 0.19-0.23 deg vs 0.13-0.15 out) at every cap. - Element decomposition: the excess lives in Delta-omega -- the in-plane argument of perihelion (p = 0.022), not Delta-Om or Delta-i. An in-plane phase shift: the clock-sector element. - Era-stable: pre-2006 comets p = 0.0095, post-2006 p = 0.10 -- present in both survey generations. - The repulsion signature: anti-cap aphelion deficit -- 6 of 54 comets within 60 deg of the anti-axis vs ~13.5 expected under isotropy (~2.4 sigma). Mirrors the TNO-side antipode deficit (step_b6) on a completely independent population. - Depth-selective: the shallow-plunger control (q >= 3.1 AU, CODE-only, class-1) is flat (p = 0.16; med 0.075 vs 0.076). Only comets that dive through the full nested structure -- boundary layers plus deep inner-system transit -- carry the anomaly. A boundary effect compounds with traversal depth; a discovery artifact would not care about q. - NG-need fraction is LOWER in-cap on CODE (7/19 vs 19/35, p = 0.26) -- the Warsaw NG-clustering did not replicate; the anomaly is in the orbit reconstruction, not in outgassing demand. - Combined significance (Fisher, two independent samples: Warsaw discovery p = 0.0089 + CODE-only confirmation p = 0.003): p ~ 3 x 10^-4 nominal. Even with a factor of ~30 for every channel explored across steps 07-12, the joint evidence sits at ~10^-2 -- and the anatomy is now the predicted one: axis-unique, spatially localized at ~60-75 deg, rotation-without-energy, in-plane element, depth-selective, era-stable, with a complementary anti-axis deficit. ### Independent axis recovery (`results/step_13_axis_convergence.json`) - A free sky scan on the CODE-only sample -- 54 comets never used to define the axis -- recovers the anomaly direction at (lam 10 deg, beta -20 deg): 24 deg from the pre-declared TNO axis, whose own uncertainty is ~21 deg. The top ten scan directions form one coherent patch centred on the boundary. The independent comet population converges on the same sky direction the detached TNOs select -- the one-map test executed on data that never touched the axis definition. - Leave-one-out over the 19 in-cap comets: p stays within [0.0013, 0.0058] under every removal. No single object carries the signal. ### Blind validation (`results/step_14_blind_validation.json`) The tests that close the remaining methodological openings -- cap selection, inherited cuts, axis seed, and look-elsewhere: - Continuous statistic, no cap, no cuts: on ALL class-1 CODE-only Oort-spike comets (n = 131, the q < 3.1 criterion dropped entirely), d_of decays monotonically with angular distance from the TNO axis -- Spearman rho = -0.24, p = 0.005. On the matched subset rho = -0.31, p = 0.021. The structure does not depend on any chosen cap radius or perihelion cut. - Permutation null: 20,000 shuffles of the aphelion-direction / d_of pairing give empirical p = 0.0064 -- the observed pairing is unusual with no distributional assumptions. - Look-elsewhere: of 2,232 directions scanned across the full sky, only 6 (0.27%) reach the observed contrast -- and all 6 lie in the single patch centred on the TNO axis. The anomaly exists in exactly one place on the sky, and it is the place the detached TNOs selected first. - Split-half blind validation (500 reps): the axis derived on a random half of the data lands within 45 deg of the TNO direction in 70% of splits (vs ~1.5% for a random direction), and the held-out half reaches p < 0.05 at 4.4x the null rate. Axis and significance derived on data that never met. - Reverse cross-validation, the honest wrinkle: Warsaw's full-arc excess peaks at the TNO axis (p = 0.0089) but shows only a positive trend at CODE's own best axis 24 deg away (p = 0.36). The two catalogues centre the patch ~24 deg apart -- inside the combined axis uncertainty (~21 deg TNO + scan step) but a reminder that the structure is a patch of finite extent, not a point. - No-q-cut cap test: with the Warsaw-inherited regime dropped entirely (all 131 class-1 CODE-only comets), the cap contrast is p = 0.010. The matched regime sharpened the signal; it did not create it. ### Model comparison and last controls (`results/step_15_model_comparison.json`) - Ecliptic-proximity confound closed: the excess holds independently in BOTH latitude strata and is stronger at high |beta| (p = 0.0017, med 0.249 vs 0.137) than at low |beta| (p = 0.031) -- the opposite ordering from deeper ecliptic-plane scattering. On a |beta|-matched subsample the contrast persists (med 0.212 vs 0.138). - A Gaussian patch model fits a characteristic width sigma ~ 68 deg -- consistent with the cap-sensitivity peak at ~60-75 deg. (The parametric fit is BIC-conservative at n = 54; the signal lives in the rank structure, where the nonparametric tests carry it.) - Quality-class independence: with ALL orbit classes admitted (n = 151 CODE-only Oort-spike comets), the cap contrast is p = 0.033. The anomaly lives in the sky direction, not in orbit quality. (Class-2/3 subsample too sparse in-cap to test alone: n_in = 3.) - Era control: d_of shows no drift with perihelion year (rho = -0.01, p = 0.91) -- not a survey-generation or modelling-era artifact. ### Signed channels and the tightest control (`results/step_16_signed_channels.json`) - The doubly-matched test -- each in-cap comet paired with the out-cap comet nearest in BOTH perihelion depth q and realized energy kick |Delta(1/a)|: the rotation excess not only survives but strengthens -- med 0.212 vs 0.088 deg, p = 0.0019 over 19 pairs. Perihelion depth and kick magnitude held fixed pairwise; the anomaly is not encounter geometry. - Signed repulsion channel: NULL -- future aphelia do not systematically migrate away from the axis (11/19 vs 23/35, p = 0.51). The anti-cap deficit is a population asymmetry, not a measurable per-orbit push in this channel. - Rotation-axis coherence: NULL and reversed -- in-cap twist axes are LESS coherent than out-cap (R = 0.37 vs 0.59). The anomaly is a distributed perturbation of the reconstruction, not a coherent torque about a shared axis. - Joint patch fit: both catalogues' own best axes fall inside the anomaly patch (CODE at 24 deg, Warsaw at 37 deg from the TNO axis) but sit 55 deg apart -- the structure is a broad ~60-70 deg feature whose centre wanders at n ~ 50-100, not a point. Consistent with a wide boundary sector rather than a single directed line. ### The planetary baseline (`results/step_17_planetary_baseline.json`) The test the whole analysis needed: the TEP observable is the residual after the standard planetary baseline, so the baseline is bu