\delta_{ij}\,dx^i dx^j . \label{eq:lapseflrw} \end{equation} A direct symbolic evaluation of the Einstein tensor (pipeline check `S1_lapse_friedmann`) gives \begin{equation} G_{00} = 3\left(\frac{\dot a}{a}\right)^{\!2}, \label{eq:g00} \end{equation} which contains no lapse at all. The lapse enters only through the matter side: for comoving dust $T_{00}=\rho N^2$, so that Einstein's equation $G_{00}=8\pi G\,T_{00}$ returns the proper-time Friedmann equation \begin{equation} \left(\frac{\dot a}{aN}\right)^{2}=\frac{8\pi G\rho}{3}. \end{equation} The geometric equation knows nothing about which clock measures time; the entire content of the first Friedmann equation is the relation between *coordinate-time* expansion and density, with the choice of clock residing on the stress-tensor side. Friedmann's $N\equiv1$ is a gauge fixing — legitimate as a coordinate choice, but it silently promotes one clock convention to the universal physical one. Under reparametrisation $t\to T(t)$, $N$ transforms so that $N\,dt$ is invariant; nothing physical is fixed by the choice. The pipeline verifies that photon redshift in the metric \eqref{eq:lapseflrw} is independent of $N(t)$ to $2\times10^{-16}$ (check `N1_lapse_gauge`): for arbitrary profiles $N(t)=1$, $1+0.5\sin 3t$, $e^{0.3t}$, the relation $1+z=a_0/a_e$ is recovered identically. ### 2.3 The Weyl postulate is the fatal step The deeper assumption is F2. The Weyl postulate asserts that the matter content of the universe defines a single privileged time congruence — all fundamental observers share one proper-time rate. This is what permits a single function $a(t)$ to represent the state of the universe. It is an assumption about clocks, and it is exactly what a dynamical time field denies. Under the disformal coupling \eqref{eq:disformal} with a homogeneous $\phi(t)$, the matter-frame lapse is \begin{equation} \tilde N^2 = A(\phi)^2 N^2 - B(\phi)\,\dot\phi^{\,2}, \label{eq:disformal_lapse} \end{equation} which is field-dependent. Imposing the Friedmann gauge $\tilde N\equiv1$ constrains the field to satisfy $\dot\phi^{\,2} = (A^2-1)/B$ (verified symbolically, `S4_disformal_lapse`): the Weyl condition is reachable only along a measure-zero family of field trajectories. Generically — that is, for almost all configurations of a dynamical clock field — no global synchronous gauge exists, and the premise on which the Friedmann solution class is constructed is unavailable. The expansion framework is not contradicted by the field; it is revealed as the special case in which the field has been tuned so that its lapse effect vanishes identically. ### 2.4 Findings The audit produces five findings, each machine-verified: - **E1 (structural).** The Friedmann framework has no slot for a physical lapse field; the Weyl postulate removes the degree of freedom that TEP identifies as the carrier of redshift. - **E2 (identification).** The kinematic reading $1+z=a_0/a_e$ is one of two observationally equivalent assignments of the same conformal observable; Section 3 constructs the other. - **E3 (rescue).** The framework's only handle on the observed $r(z)$ is the matter content; when the matter-only model fails, the failure can only be absorbed by inventing a new energy component — $\Lambda$ — rather than by revising the identification (Section 7.2 quantifies the failure). - **E4 (ontology).** The Big-Bang singularity is an inference within one conformal frame; in the conformal frame where space is static the same solution has vanishing curvature invariants (check `N5_singularity_frame`). - **E5 (consistency).** The framework's own parameters, fitted to different redshift windows of the same real data, drift with redshift — the internal signature of a misassigned observable (Section 8.2). ## 3. The conformal degeneracy theorem ### 3.1 Statement **Theorem (homogeneous-sector degeneracy).** Let $\mathcal{O}=\{z(r),\,D_L(z),\,D_A(z),\,\text{dilation}(z),\,\text{SB}(z),\,\eta(z)\}$ be the set of homogeneous background observables of any FLRW model $a(t)$. Then there exists a static-frame representation in which the auxiliary spatial geometry is fixed and the same set $\mathcal{O}$ is generated pointwise by a scalar clock-rate profile $A(\eta)$. ### 3.2 Construction Write the static-frame line element in conformal form \begin{equation} ds^2 = A(\eta)^2\left(-d\eta^2 + d\vec x^{\,2}\right). \label{eq:staticconformal} \end{equation} Null rays satisfy $x=\eta+\text{const}$, so the path distance is $r=\eta_0-\eta_e$, and a static observer $u^\mu=(A^{-1},\vec 0)$ measures photon frequency $\omega = -k\cdot u = p/A(\eta)$ with conserved comoving momentum $p$; hence \begin{equation} 1+z = \frac{A(\eta_0)}{A(\eta_e)}. \label{eq:staticz} \end{equation} Given *any* measured relation $r_{\rm target}(z)$, place the observer at $\eta_0 = r_{\rm target}(z_{\max})$ and define \begin{equation} A(\eta) = \frac{A_0}{1+z(\eta_0-\eta)}, \label{eq:construction} \end{equation} with $z(r)$ the inverse of $r_{\rm target}$. The construction is algebraic and exact. Photon number conservation in any metric theory gives the Etherington relation $D_L=(1+z)^2 D_A$ with $D_A=r/(1+z)$, hence $D_L=r(1+z)$; arrival rates and surface-brightness scalings follow identically. ### 3.3 Verification The pipeline verifies the theorem two ways. First, the analytic frequency law \eqref{eq:staticz} is checked by direct path integration in the static-lapse metric (`N2_static_lapse_redshift`, residual $9\times10^{-16}$). Second, and materially stronger, the full null geodesic equation is integrated numerically through a metric built by \eqref{eq:construction} from the best-fit $\Lambda$CDM distance-redshift relation; the redshift recovered along the integrated ray reproduces the target $z(r)$ to $5.7\times10^{-15}$ absolute and the path-distance relation to $3.0\times10^{-11}$ relative (`N3_constructive_degeneracy`). The same step verifies the observable identities — $\eta(z)=1$, Tolman exponent exactly 4, time dilation exactly $1+z$ (`N4_metric_redshift_identities`) — and extends the coverage to the first time-domain observable: the Sandage–Loeb drift of a fixed-comoving source obeys the identical identity $\dot z=(1+z)H_{\rm meas}(0)-H_{\rm meas}(z)$ in the static frame, with the measured rate given by the running clock field $H_{\rm meas}=A'/A^2$ (`N6_sandage_loeb_static`, residual $2.6\times10^{-9}$). The drift is therefore not a frame marker — it reads the transport law directly, which is what makes it decisive in Section 9.3. ### 3.4 Consequences Three consequences follow immediately. (i) *The classical expansion proofs test metric redshift, not expansion.* Supernova light-curve stretching $\propto(1+z)$, the Tolman $(1+z)^{-4}$ surface-brightness law, and distance-duality consistency are reproduced identically by the static temporal-shear frame. They confirm that cosmological redshift is a metric phenomenon — they do not, and cannot, select its spatial versus temporal assignment. (ii) *Expansion is a coordinate ontology.* The pure-conformal subsector of TEP is literally the FLRW metric written in conformal form; on the Pantheon+SH0ES catalogue the pure-conformal model reproduces the $\Lambda$CDM distance-modulus vector with a maximum absolute residual of exactly $0.0$ (`PC_conformal`, Section 7.1). Nothing in homogeneous background data separates "space expands" from "the clock field runs". (iii) *What the data can decide is the shape of the profile.* The observational question is not *whether* the universe expands but *which profile* the temporal transport follows — the FLRW reading forces the profile into the rigid form dictated by a conserved-fluid Friedmann equation, while the physical TEP branch allows the profile its own dynamics. That is the testable content, and it is where the models are compared in Sections 6–7. ## 4. The disformal breaking: where the readings separate The degeneracy theorem holds exactly for the pure-conformal subsector ($B=0$). The full TEP coupling \eqref{eq:disformal} breaks it: the shear sector makes the transport history a physical degree of freedom rather than a coordinate repackaging. In the corpus parametrisation (TEP-C0, `core/cosmology.py`), the physical no-$\Lambda$ branch — denoted TEP-M1 — is a matter-only background $\Omega_m=1$ whose measured transport rate carries the temporal-shear factor \begin{equation} \gamma(z) = 1 + \epsilon_T\,\ln(1+z)\,e^{-(z/z_T)^{n_T}},\qquad n_T=1, \label{eq:gamma} \end{equation} so that the measured expansion-rate and path-distance observables become \begin{equation} H_{\rm TEP}(z) = \frac{H_0\,(1+z)^{3/2}}{\gamma(z)},\qquad D^{\rm TEP}(z) = \frac{c}{H_0}\!\int_0^z \frac{\gamma(z')}{(1+z')^{3/2}}\,dz', \label{eq:tepobs} \end{equation} with $D_L = D(1+z)$, $D_A = D/(1+z)$ as required by Etherington. Two properties deserve emphasis. First, $\gamma>1$ lengthens the accumulated path at intermediate $z$ relative to the EdS path — the same phenomenology that, read through the Friedmann identification, is named "dark energy". Second, $\gamma$ is not a substance: it is the running of the clock field, with an independently specified density-dependent screening law (TEP-C0, Section 2.5) that ties cosmological shear to local environments. The model's content is $\Omega_m=1$ plus one scalar field sector; $\Lambda$CDM's content is $\Omega_m\approx0.3$ matter plus $\Omega_\Lambda\approx0.7$ of a substance with $w=-1$ that has never been independently detected. ## 5. Data Four independent real datasets are used, staged by `step_00` with SHA-256 provenance (Appendix B): 1. **Pantheon+SH0ES** — 1,701 SNe Ia, $0.001<z<2.26$, with the full $1701\times1701$ statistical-plus-systematic covariance matrix [4,5]. The apparent-magnitude column `m_b_corr` and CMB-frame redshift `zCMB` are used throughout. 2. **Cosmic chronometers** — 33 measurements of $H(z)$ from differential galaxy ages, $z=0.07$–$1.965$ (Moresco et al. 2022 compilation [6]). 3. **Uncorrelated BAO** — 17 measurements from the Benisty–Staicova compilation (arXiv:2009.10701 [7]), mixing $D_V/r_s$, $D_V$, $D_A/r_s$, $D_A$, $r_s/D_V$, $H\,r_s$ and $D_H/r_s$ observables over $z=0.106$–$2.34$. The sound horizon $r_s=147.1$ Mpc is held fixed for every model — TEP-HC establishes acoustic-scale preservation $r_s^{\rm TEP}/r_s^{\Lambda{\rm CDM}}=0.999994$, so no model is given a hidden degree of freedom. 4. **DESI DR1 BAO** — 12 measurements in 7 redshift bins, $z=0.295$–$2.33$ (DESI Collaboration 2024, arXiv:2404.03002, Table 1 [11]), transcribed verbatim including the published $D_M/r_d$–$D_H/r_d$ correlation coefficients, which are used as proper $2\times2$ covariance blocks. This dataset is deliberately held out of the step-05 joint inference: it serves as an independent validation set from a different survey and a different era (Section 7.5). ## 6. Methods Models compared: $\Lambda$CDM (flat), EdS ($\Omega_m=1$: the original matter-only Friedmann model), $w$CDM (flat, constant-$w$ dark energy), TEP-M1 (Eq. \ref{eq:tepobs}), and the pure-conformal PC model as a degeneracy control. In `step_08` the expansion side is additionally given its maximal standard freedom: $w_0w_a$CDM (CPL parametrisation, $k=5$) and non-flat o$\Lambda$CDM ($k=4$, with the curvature factor $S_k$ applied to every transverse distance) enter the same joint fit. Two frame-independent geometric tests are applied in the same step: an Etherington exponent $\eta(z)=(1+z)^\beta$ multiplying the supernova luminosity distance is fitted at likelihood level on both the $\Lambda$CDM and TEP-M1 backgrounds, and independently reconstructed at the BAO angular-diameter redshifts using a model-free inverse-$E$ polynomial interpolant of the supernova Hubble diagram; and the Alcock–Paczyński ratio $F_{\rm AP}=D_M/D_H$ — which cancels $r_d$ and $H_0$ identically — is evaluated on the five paired DESI DR1 bins with the published $D_M/r_d$–$D_H/r_d$ correlations propagated into the $F_{\rm AP}$ errors. All $\chi^2$ are computed against the data as released: for the SNe, $\chi^2=\vec r\,C^{-1}\vec r^{\,\top}$ with the full covariance and the offset $\mathcal M$ either fitted or analytically marginalised (reported both ways in the step outputs); for DESI, the published intra-bin $D_M/r_d$–$D_H/r_d$ correlation coefficients are assembled into a block-diagonal covariance and the same form is used. Joint inference uses a single parameter vector per model across all three probes — $H_0$ constrained by chronometers and BAO, the SN shape by the Hubble flow, $\mathcal M$ absorbing the $H_0\mathcal M_B$ degeneracy. Bayesian evidence is estimated by the Laplace approximation at the joint maximum for all models and by `dynesty` nested sampling (random-walk, 300 live points, $\mathrm{d}\log Z=0.5$, seeded) for the three competitive models $\Lambda$CDM, $w$CDM and TEP-M1. Robustness is checked three ways in `step_09`: the chronometer errors are inflated ($\times1.5$, $\times2$) and given a $5\%$ fractional systematic floor before repeating the joint fit; the Laplace Bayes factor is recomputed on a $3\times3$ grid of TEP prior boxes ($\epsilon_T^{\max}\in\{5,10,20\}$, $z_T^{\max}\in\{50,300,1000\}$); and the joint inference is extended to include the DESI DR1 holdout set. A final pipeline step (`step_10_claims_audit`) verifies every headline number quoted in this paper against the machine-readable outputs in `results/` — the manuscript cannot silently diverge from the data products. ## 7. Results ### 7.1 The degeneracy control The pure-conformal model returns the $\Lambda$CDM $\chi^2$ bit-for-bit: $\chi^2_{\rm PC}=1989.00=\chi^2_{\Lambda{\rm CDM}}$, with a maximum absolute distance-modulus residual of $0.0$ across the catalogue. This is the degeneracy theorem executed on real data: the same numbers, two ontologies. ### 7.2 The matter-only Friedmann model fails absolutely EdS — the model Friedmann's framework actually delivers, before interpretive rescue — is falsified by every probe: | Probe | $\chi^2_{\rm EdS}$ | $\chi^2_{\Lambda{\rm CDM}}$ | $\Delta\chi^2$ | significance | |-------|--------------------|-----------------------------|----------------|--------------| | Cosmic chronometers (33) | 50.69 | 14.56 | $+36.13$ | $6.0\sigma$ | | Pantheon+SH0ES (1701) | 2571.89 | 1989.00 | $+582.89$ | $24\sigma$ | | BAO (17) | 324.97 | 8.72 | $+316.25$ | $18\sigma$ | | **Joint** | **2955.38** | **2023.85** | **$+931.53$** | $\sim30\sigma$ | Inside the Friedmann framework there is exactly one remedy, because the framework has no other degree of freedom: assign $70\%$ of the universe's content to an undetected negative-pressure component. The EdS column is what the unrescued theory predicts; the gap between the columns is the precise quantitative size of the $\Lambda$ epicycle. The age ledger sharpens the point: integrated lookback gives $t_0 = 9.31$ Gyr for EdS at $H_0=70$ — younger than the $12.5$ Gyr globular-cluster floor — and even at $H_0=50$ only $13.04$ Gyr, against $13.47$ Gyr for $\Lambda$CDM at $H_0=70$, $\Omega_m=0.3$. The matter-only Friedmann model is falsified on grounds internal to the framework, independently of any TEP argument. ### 7.3 The temporal-shear branch outperforms ΛCDM on the joint dataset | Model | $k$ | $\chi^2_{\rm CC}$ | $\chi^2_{\rm SN}$ | $\chi^2_{\rm BAO}$ | $\chi^2_{\rm joint}$ | BIC$_{\rm joint}$ | |-------|-----|-------------------|-------------------|--------------------|----------------------|-------------------| | $\Lambda$CDM | 3 | 14.56 | 1989.00 | 8.72 | 2023.85 | 2046.26 | | EdS | 2 | 50.69 | 2571.89 | 324.97 | 2955.38 | 2970.31 | | $w$CDM | 4 | 14.42 | 1979.65 | 8.00 | 2005.38 | 2035.25 | | TEP-M1 | 4 | 14.66 | 1981.31 | 7.94 | 2007.33 | 2037.21 | ($k$ counts joint parameters; per-probe $\chi^2$ are evaluated at each probe's own best fit, the joint column at the joint best fit.) The headline numbers: - TEP-M1 improves on $\Lambda$CDM by $\Delta\chi^2=-16.52$ ($\Delta{\rm BIC}=-9.05$) on the joint dataset — with matter-only content $\Omega_m=1$ and *no dark-energy substance*. It sits at statistical parity with the best expansion model in the set: $w$CDM, which has a free dark-energy equation of state, is ahead by only $\Delta\chi^2=-2.0$ while requiring an undetected fluid. - On BAO it is the best-fitting model outright ($\chi^2=7.94$, better than $w$CDM's 8.00 and $\Lambda$CDM's 8.72), with $\epsilon_T=1.00$, $z_T=5.42$. - On the SN catalogue it beats $\Lambda$CDM by $\Delta\chi^2=-7.69$, preferring the near-unscreened regime ($z_T\to$ large; the $\gamma$ profile approaches pure logarithmic shear). - On chronometers it is statistically indistinguishable from $\Lambda$CDM ($\Delta\chi^2=+0.10$). Nested sampling (`dynesty`, 300 live points, seeded for reproducibility) gives \begin{equation} \log Z_{w{\rm CDM}} = -1018.07\pm0.63,\qquad \log Z_{\rm TEP} = -1021.12\pm0.68,\qquad \log Z_{\Lambda{\rm CDM}} = -1024.29\pm0.58, \end{equation} hence \begin{equation} \ln B_{\rm TEP/\Lambda CDM} = +3.17,\qquad \ln B_{w{\rm CDM}/\Lambda{\rm CDM}} = +6.22,\qquad \ln B_{\rm TEP/}w{\rm CDM} = -3.05. \end{equation} The no-$\Lambda$, static-space model is preferred over the concordance model by $B\approx24$ — strong evidence on the Jeffreys scale, confirmed independently by the Laplace estimate ($\log_{10}B=+1.01$, $B\approx10.1$); the sign of the preference is stable across sampler realisations — while the free-$w$ extension of the expansion framework retains the strongest evidence overall. The honest reading of the full table is that the data demand an extra degree of freedom beyond $\Lambda$CDM, and both the temporal-shear sector and an effective dark-energy equation of state supply it at comparable quality; only the temporal-shear option does so without an undetected substance. A model containing no dark energy and no expansion outranks the model that is standardly cited as the strongest evidence for both. The preference is not an artefact of analysis choices: it survives chronometer-error inflation, a nine-box prior