mbda$CDM | 2 | 12.81 | 0 | | EdS | 1 | 496.33 | $+483.52$ | | $w$CDM | 3 | 12.77 | $-0.04$ | | TEP-M1 | 3 | 12.38 | $-0.43$ | The matter-only Friedmann model is rejected on DESI as well ($\sim22\sigma$ equivalent), while the temporal-shear branch edges out both expansion models and returns parameters coherent with the other probes: $\epsilon_T=1.13$, $z_T=4.21$, $H_0=69.29$ — the same $\epsilon_T\approx1$ shear amplitude measured independently by chronometers (1.21), the BAO compilation (1.00) and the joint fit (0.87). (b) *Frozen-parameter holdout.* The step-05 joint best fits — set entirely by chronometers, SNe and the older BAO compilation — are applied to DESI with no refitting: | Model | $\chi^2_{\rm holdout}$ | |-------|------------------------| | $\Lambda$CDM | 21.43 | | EdS | 557.27 | | $w$CDM | 17.01 | | TEP-M1 | 18.20 | Both non-$\Lambda$ models predict the unseen DESI measurements better than the concordance model does ($\Delta\chi^2=-3.22$ for TEP-M1, $-4.42$ for $w$CDM): the predictive content of the extra degree of freedom generalises across instruments. (c) *Extended joint.* Adding DESI to the joint likelihood and refitting gives, on 1,763 data points: $\chi^2_{\rm TEP}=2023.30$, $\chi^2_{w{\rm CDM}}=2020.69$, $\chi^2_{\Lambda{\rm CDM}}=2041.52$, $\chi^2_{\rm EdS}=3471.59$ — i.e. $\Delta\chi^2_{\rm TEP-LCDM}=-18.22$ and $\Delta{\rm BIC}_{\rm TEP-LCDM}=-10.74$. The TEP-M1 advantage over $\Lambda$CDM grows when the newest BAO data are included rather than shrinking toward it. ### 7.6 Robustness of the inference Two systematic objections are checked directly. *Chronometer systematics.* The joint fit is repeated with the chronometer errors inflated by $\times1.5$ and $\times2$, and with a $5\%$ fractional systematic floor added in quadrature. The TEP-M1 advantage is stable: $\Delta\chi^2_{\rm TEP-LCDM}$ moves from $-16.52$ (baseline) to $-16.76$, $-16.85$ and $-16.58$ respectively — the comparison remains negative in all variants and the magnitude even grows slightly as the SN and BAO data carry more relative weight. The EdS rejection survives identically ($\Delta\chi^2\geq+898$ in every variant). *Prior-volume sensitivity.* Bayes factors depend on prior boxes; the Laplace evidence ratio is therefore recomputed on a $3\times3$ grid of TEP prior volumes ($\epsilon_T^{\max}\in\{5,10,20\}$, $z_T^{\max}\in\{50,300,1000\}$). The preference for TEP-M1 over $\Lambda$CDM is positive in all nine boxes, $\log_{10}B\in[+0.18,+2.09]$: enlarging the $z_T$ box costs the model evidence exactly as the Occam penalty predicts, yet never reverses the preference. The nested-sampling value $\ln B=+3.17$ sits inside this range and is not a tuned-prior artefact. ### 7.7 The expansion framework at maximal freedom The contest of Section 7.3 grants expansion only a single dark-energy parameter. The fair question is whether the comparison survives when the expansion framework is given its full standard freedom. The two canonical extensions are therefore added to the identical joint fit: $w_0w_a$CDM, freeing the dark-energy equation of state to cross the phantom divide, and o$\Lambda$CDM, freeing spatial curvature. | Model | $k$ | $\chi^2_{\rm joint}$ | AIC | BIC | $\log_{10}B_{\rm Lap}$ vs TEP-M1 | |-------|-----|----------------------|-----|-----|-----| | EdS | 2 | 2955.38 | 2959.38 | 2970.31 | $-202.18$ | | $\Lambda$CDM | 3 | 2023.85 | 2029.85 | 2046.26 | $-1.01$ | | $w$CDM | 4 | 2005.38 | 2013.38 | 2035.25 | $+1.76$ | | o$\Lambda$CDM | 4 | 2005.80 | 2013.80 | 2035.67 | $+2.30$ | | TEP-M1 | 4 | 2007.33 | 2015.33 | 2037.21 | $0$ | | $w_0w_a$CDM | 5 | 2001.51 | 2011.51 | 2038.85 | $+1.91$ | The enlarged set remains inside the equivalence class: the constructive degeneracy of Section 3, verified on the best-fit radial profile of each new model, reproduces the phantom-crossing $w_0w_a$CDM and curved o$\Lambda$CDM distance laws in the static frame to $3\times10^{-15}$ — maximal freedom enlarges the class without buying uniqueness. Three facts fo